/* =====================================================================
   REFERENCE LIBRARY - games that demonstrably succeeded, dissected with
   one template so an idea can be cross-referenced against them.
   Entries describe design mechanisms, not sales figures. "Why it worked"
   is the widely reported explanation from postmortems, interviews and
   critical consensus; treat it as a strong heuristic, not a proof.
   Template: want served · core verb · first 30 seconds · decision per minute ·
   engines · why it worked · what players complain about · lesson · what copies miss
   ===================================================================== */
const REFERENCE_GAMES = [
  { id:'slay-the-spire', img:'assets/games/slay-the-spire.jpg', t:'Slay the Spire', year:2019, genre:'roguelike deckbuilder', want:'Build something clever from what I am handed and see it pay off. No two runs the same.', verb:'choose a card to add, then play the hand you drew', first30:'You start Act 1 with a fixed starter deck of Strikes, Defends and one or two signature cards and a first fight against weak, readable enemies whose next move already shows above their head. Winning gives a choice of one card from three; skipping is always an option. Before floor one, Neow offers a choice of blessings.', minute:'Take this card, or skip it to keep the deck lean? Play it now for damage, or hold energy to block the attack you can see coming? Push to the next node, or duck into a rest site to heal or upgrade a card instead?', engines:['mastery','expression','discovery'], why:'Every reward is a decision, not a stat increase: taking a card is also a bet about what your deck will need three fights later, and skipping keeps a deck small enough to draw well. Enemy intent icons show the next attack’s type and exact damage before you commit a card, so a loss reads as a misjudged trade-off rather than bad luck. Mega Crit backed this up with data: a metrics server tracked how often each card was picked and how often it appeared in winning decks throughout Early Access, catching problems such as an early Dual Wield that could copy any card in hand for infinite combos. Cards were still checked against how they felt to play, not the numbers alone, before anything shipped.', complaints:'Runs can feel decided by the first few offers: a bad early choice of cards or relics is hard to recover from, and some archetypes have arguably dominated patches until the next rebalance. New players can find Ascension’s twenty stacking modifiers and the run’s vocabulary of icons overwhelming before they have seen enough cards to plan around them.', lesson:'Make the reward itself the decision, and show enough information (an enemy’s next move, a card’s exact numbers) that a loss can be traced to a specific choice. Keep the rule set small enough that a player can learn all of it, then let a metrics pipeline and an active community catch what design intuition missed after launch.', misses:'Copies add more cards and relics without asking whether each one creates a new situational decision, and many skip the readability budget: no consistent enemy-intent icon, so a loss reads as bad luck rather than a misplay. Few keep rebalancing after launch the way Mega Crit tracked card pick rates and win rates for a full year of Early Access.' },
  { id:'stardew-valley', img:'assets/games/stardew-valley.jpg', t:'Stardew Valley', year:2016, genre:'farming and life sim', want:'A place of my own that grows because I tended it, with people who notice me, at my own pace.', verb:'tend: plant, water, gather, give', first30:'You wake on your first morning already owning a rundown farm: a hoe, a watering can, an axe, a pickaxe, a scythe and fifteen parsnip seeds from the mayor, and the whole day free to spend. A letter from your late grandfather explains why you left the city. A walk east into Pelican Town shows a general store, a saloon, a derelict Community Center and neighbours who already have names and routines of their own.', minute:'Water this row now, or risk a wilted crop tomorrow? Chop the tree blocking a path, or leave it for the sap? Push one more level into the mine, or turn back before energy runs out and collapsing costs gold? Give today’s spare parsnip to a villager not yet visited this week, or sell it and bank the coins instead? Every choice spends one of two budgets: the hours before 2am, or the energy before collapse.', engines:['progression','expression','social','collection'], why:'Every day hands the player a fresh, small budget of time and energy to spend on visible, compounding improvements: a watered field, a levelled skill, a gift given. Eric Barone built the whole game alone over about four and a half years to fix what he felt Harvest Moon never quite got right, and the result arguably answers that genre’s old frictions: a hospital stay softened to a capped cash fine, a marriage pool opened to any of twelve candidates regardless of gender, and no two-year ending. The farm’s own visible neglect at the start briefs the player on the job without a tutorial, and the optional Community Center-or-JojaMart choice later lets that same patient, generous design ask a real question about how the player wants to grow the town.', complaints:'The game gives almost no upfront guidance, so systems such as gift preferences, bundle contents and skill perks are usually learned from a wiki rather than from the game itself. Some players find the daily energy and clock pressure stressful even though its penalty is capped and mild. Combat is simple next to the farming and social systems, and a late-game farm with maxed skills and deep savings can make further money-earning feel pointless.', lesson:'Give the player a small, renewing budget with many good uses, cap the cost of a bad day so mistakes teach rather than compound, and let the starting state of the world itself explain the job. Where an older genre entry had a real complaint, harsh penalties, a closed marriage pool, no post-launch support, answer it specifically rather than reinventing the whole design, and keep answering it for years after launch.', misses:'Copies ship the watering-and-selling loop and the seasonal calendar without the density of things that notice the player: named villagers with schedules and gift preferences, festivals on fixed calendar days, a farm that visibly remembers what was cleared and built. Others copy the daily timer without capping its penalty, so a bad day punishes far harder than Stardew Valley’s capped, mostly-monetary cost for collapsing.' },
  { id:'vampire-survivors', img:'assets/games/vampire-survivors.jpg', t:'Vampire Survivors', year:2022, genre:'auto-shooter survival roguelite', want:'Feel my power snowball while doing almost nothing, in ten minutes, with a level-up choice every few seconds.', verb:'move to survive. Choose an upgrade on every level-up', first30:'You start in an open field with one weapon already firing on its own; the only input is a direction to walk. Bats and zombies close in from every side within seconds, gems drop as they die, and the first level-up screen appears before a minute has passed, offering a choice of three or four weapons and passives with no tutorial text explaining any of them.', minute:'Which of these three or four cards helps the build already running: more damage, a new weapon, or the passive this build’s evolution needs? Is it safe to detour towards a distant cluster of gems, or does the crowd behind cut off the way back? Open this chest now, mid-swarm, or lure enemies away first? And with the clock creeping towards thirty minutes, is this build strong enough to survive what happens then?', engines:['power','progression','collection','optimization'], why:'Removing aim and timing from combat leaves only two decisions, where to walk and what to pick at level-up, so the whole game reads as legible even at full screen-filling chaos: every choice is a menu, not a reflex test. Luca Galante built it around a purchased Castlevania-styled asset pack rather than commissioned art, which let the game reach Steam Early Access in December 2021 at three dollars and grow from a few thousand players to over seventy thousand concurrent within two months largely on word of mouth. A run’s own ending is designed as a reward rather than a defeat: surviving to the thirty-minute mark summons a nearly unkillable Reaper whose killing blow is logged as a stage complete, not a loss, turning the game’s own final death into a win condition.', complaints:'The screen becomes close to unreadable in a long run’s final minutes, when hundreds of enemies and effects overlap at once. Runs converge on a small number of known-strong weapon evolutions once players learn which passive pairs with which weapon, since the game lists these pairings only once found, in its Grimoire. Some players see the level-up-and-auto-attack loop as closer to a slot machine than a game asking for skill.', lesson:'A genre’s execution and its decisions can be separated: automating the reflex half of combat and keeping only frequent, legible choices can open a demanding genre to a much larger audience. Treat even a run’s ending as a design opportunity rather than only a failure state, and a cheap, honestly priced, frequently updated release can outcompete a bigger production largely through word of mouth.', misses:'Copies add more weapons, characters and biomes but keep the same level-up choices without checking whether each one still feels consequential once players have seen every option, so the decision that made the original legible becomes rote. Others keep the chaos and the auto-attacking but drop the clear, escalating end point the Reaper provides, leaving a run to simply stop rather than end on the design’s own terms.' },
  { id:'balatro', img:'assets/games/balatro.jpg', t:'Balatro', year:2024, genre:'poker roguelike deckbuilder', want:'Break a system I already understand (poker hands) with absurd multipliers of my own making.', verb:'select cards to play or discard. Buy jokers that change the scoring rules', first30:'You are dealt a hand from an ordinary 52-card deck and shown a chip target for the small blind. A play button, a discard button and a left-hand panel of target, score, hands and discards left make up the table; anyone who has played poker already knows which hand beats which.', minute:'Play this hand now, or discard down to chase two pairs into a full house? Spend a tarot card to fix a bad draw, or save it for the shop? Rearrange the Jokers so a flat bonus fires before the multiplier that doubles it? Every hand asks which order turns a modest score into an outrageous one.', engines:['optimization','discovery','mastery','surprise'], why:'LocalThunk, an anonymous Saskatchewan developer who worked in IT and had never released a commercial game, built Balatro almost entirely alone over roughly two and a half years, hiring only a composer. The design borrows a rule set nearly every adult already holds, standard poker hands, so the game spends no time teaching what a straight or a flush is and pours its invention into how its 150 Jokers interact when stacked together. Because score is chips multiplied by mult, and Jokers fire in a chosen left-to-right order, a run becomes an exercise in arranging those interactions so a modest hand explodes into an outrageous number, echoing LocalThunk’s own stated influences, Big Cheat, a house card game built from Big Two and Cheat, and the slot-machine roguelike Luck Be a Landlord.', complaints:'Some Jokers and synergy decks are so far ahead of the field that runs can feel solved once a player knows which ones to chase, and a shop that never offers any of them can end a run that had otherwise played well. The steep jump in required score across Antes 2 to 5 punishes any build that has not started multiplying by then, and stacking five Jokers plus tarot and planet cards on screen at once can make a late hand briefly hard to read.', lesson:'Borrow a rule set the audience already owns instead of inventing one, and spend the saved teaching budget entirely on how a handful of modifiers interact and in what order. Exposing that order as something the player can rearrange turns simple stacking into a real decision, and a payoff that is visibly, almost absurdly large makes the decision worth making again.', misses:'Copies borrow the shop-and-run shape but invent their own currency of symbols and effects nobody already understands, so the teaching cost the poker hands were meant to remove comes straight back. Others let modifiers apply in a fixed, hidden order rather than one the player controls, which turns the stacking puzzle Balatro built back into a spreadsheet the game quietly solves for you.' },
  { id:'celeste', img:'assets/games/celeste.jpg', t:'Celeste', year:2018, genre:'precision platformer', want:'Prove to myself I can do the hard thing, with failure so cheap I never stop trying.', verb:'jump, dash, climb. Die and retry in under a second', first30:'The Prologue teaches jump and climbing a wall, then, as a bridge collapses, a bird teaches the dash in its final moments; Chapter 1, Forsaken City, asks Madeline to chain all three across cliffs, spikes and drops. Death respawns her instantly at the start of the current room, with no loading and almost no delay.', minute:'Which route through this screen, the safe ledge or the risky dash? Commit to the jump now, or wait one more beat for the wind-up? Detour for the strawberry sitting past a spike, or leave it and keep climbing? Every room asks the same question at a slightly harder angle.', engines:['mastery','tension','relief','storytelling'], why:'Maddy Thorson and Noel Berry built the game from a free four-day PICO-8 jam prototype in 2015 into a full release in 2018, and Thorson has since published the exact list of forgiveness techniques, coyote time, jump buffering, generous wall-jump windows, that make failure feel earned rather than cheap, in her own words fudging timing “a tiny bit in the player’s favour” throughout. Assist Mode lets a struggling player slow the game, add dashes or turn on invincibility and, since a 2019 update, without branding the save file, so the same demanding platforming stays reachable for players who cannot clear it unaided. Thorson has said the story, and Badeline as a manifestation of anxiety, grew out of her own experience, and Lena Raine’s adaptive score unlocks more musical layers as the player progresses through a chapter rather than looping one fixed cue.', complaints:'Some late B-Sides and C-Sides demand execution far beyond the main story, so completionists chasing every cassette and crystal heart meet a much harder game than the one most players finished. The instant, frequent deaths that make failure cheap can still tally into the hundreds or thousands across a save, a number some players find discouraging to see counted. Assist Mode’s own launch text, which called the game’s difficulty “essential”, was criticised as undercutting the feature and was rewritten in 2019.', lesson:'Hide generous timing windows under a difficult game, coyote time, buffered inputs, wide wall-jump ranges, so a near-miss lands and failure always traces back to the player’s own read of a fixed, learnable room; a game can be very hard and very fair at once. Let players adjust execution demands without asking anyone else’s permission or changing what the story shows them.', misses:'Copies ship instant respawns and hard rooms but skip the specific forgiveness techniques, generous jump-buffer and wall-jump windows, that make Celeste’s difficulty feel earned rather than arbitrary, so a room that should read as a fair puzzle instead reads as a twitch-reflex check. Others bolt on a single binary easy mode rather than Celeste’s granular Assist Mode, which lets a player remove exactly the friction they personally struggle with and nothing else.' },
  { id:'hades', img:'assets/games/hades.jpg', t:'Hades', year:2020, genre:'action roguelike', want:'Get better at a fight that feels great, while the story rewards me for dying.', verb:'attack, dash, special, cast. Choose a reward at every door', first30:'You start already mid-escape in Tartarus, Stygian Blade in hand, and the first chamber hands you a boon from an Olympian before the first fight begins. When you die, and early on you will, you surface from the Pool of Styx in the House of Hades, where Hypnos greets you with a quip about what killed you, and Nyx, Achilles and Hades himself are a few steps away.', minute:'Which boon fits the build you are chasing, Ares’ Doom or Poseidon’s knockback? Take the door that promises a Chthonic Key for the Mirror, or the Centaur Heart you need to survive this run? Spend Charon’s obols at the next shop, since death takes them, or save them for a better offer?', engines:['mastery','storytelling','progression','expression'], why:'Supergiant aimed Hades at the problem this genre has always had: dying, over and over. Rather than hide failure, every death is a scene, with named characters in the House of Hades who remember your last run, your last weapon, even which god you favour, because Supergiant scripted call-and-response dialogue keyed to many specific states rather than one generic loss screen. Early Access, first on the Epic Games Store from December 2018 and later on Steam from December 2019, let the studio add gods, weapons and story content in stages and watch which boons and builds players reached for before the story or the balance were finished; the true ending only arrived with the version 1.0 launch on 17 September 2020, alongside new events for all thirty voiced characters.', complaints:'Some players find that once enough Mirror of Night talents are bought, or God Mode is switched on in the settings, a run stops feeling dangerous, since its damage resistance grows with every death rather than staying fixed. Others feel the Pact of Punishment’s Heat modifiers mostly add tougher enemies and one-shot risks rather than new situations, so climbing Heat can read as a grind for rewards more than a fresh challenge, and returning to a cleared weapon aspect after the credits can feel like homework rather than a new escape attempt.', lesson:'Turn the moment players usually quit, losing, into the moment they most want to keep playing, by making it advance a relationship rather than reset one. Track state precisely enough, which weapon, which god, how far, who died to what, that a character can respond to this specific failure rather than failure in general, and let every run bank part of its haul, Darkness for the Mirror, keys, gems, Nectar, so a loss still buys something permanent.', misses:'Copies add a hub with voiced characters but write only a handful of generic lines that repeat within a few hours, so returning to talk stops paying off long before the player stops dying. Others bolt on a meta-progression currency without touching the writing at all, so the world never seems to notice how many times you have died, or which of its own gods you keep coming back to for boons.' },
  { id:'into-the-breach', img:'assets/games/into-the-breach.jpg', t:'Into the Breach', year:2018, genre:'tactics', want:'Solve a small, fully readable puzzle under stakes, where I can see every consequence before I act.', verb:'position three mechs so enemy attacks miss, hit each other, or hit nothing', first30:'An 8×8 grid, three mechs, and a handful of alien Vek already marked with the tile and direction of the attack each will make next turn. An objectives panel states the goal: protect the buildings, not just survive the fight. Every queued Vek attack is shown, so the first decision you make is already an informed one, not a guess dressed up as a plan.', minute:'Push the Vek off the tile it is about to hit, or let the attack land somewhere less costly? Block the tile a spawning Vek would land on, or leave a mech free to fire this turn? Take damage on a mech you can repair by idling next turn, or spend that turn protecting the building instead? Every choice trades one visible, already-known consequence against another.', engines:['mastery','tension','optimization'], why:'Perfect information changes what a turn asks of the player: since every Vek attack for the coming turn is shown in advance and weapons never miss, a lost mech becomes a specific, nameable misread of the board rather than an unlucky roll. Justin Ma has said this was the deliberate goal, wanting every death to feel like the player’s own fault rather than a random one. The fixed 8×8 grid and three-mech squad keep the whole puzzle visible at once, so difficulty comes from how many things are worth protecting at the same time, not from numbers the player has to memorise or guess at. Development ran to about four years, and Subset Games’s own GDC talk on the game is officially described as covering years of cut content and iteration, a route the studio used to keep only the ideas that served the core decision loop rather than every idea it tried.', complaints:'Reviewers have said a player who only wants to finish can be done with Into the Breach in a couple of hours, which that reviewer felt undersells the game to anyone who does not dig deeper. One reviewer found the Rift Walkers starting squad not “even that interesting”, whatever its use in teaching, and never grasped how to make Blitzkrieg work. The story, told in short pilot and zone-leader lines rather than cutscenes, has “no definitive finale”, one critic notes.', lesson:'Remove chance from the parts of a system that would otherwise punish a good plan for no reason, and put everything the enemy will do next turn where the player can already see it: attack lines, target tiles, and damage numbers that never change once shown. Keep the board small enough that the whole battlefield stays visible in one glance, and let failure carry a real cost, a lost timeline, a reset city, without deleting everything the player has learned, so players keep choosing to try again rather than walking away from a wipe.', misses:'Copies that add visible enemy intent but keep a hit-chance percentage on the player’s own weapons give players a warning they cannot fully trust, which quietly brings back the guesswork the telegraph was supposed to remove. Others keep the small grid and the telegraphs but drop the building-as-health-pool objective, so there is nothing left to trade a mech’s safety against, and every turn collapses into simply killing whatever threatens the mechs. A few also copy the difficulty without Into the Breach’s narrow, forgiving failure state, so a single lost run costs everything rather than one timeline.' },
  { id:'minecraft', img:'assets/games/minecraft.jpg', t:'Minecraft', year:2011, genre:'sandbox survival', want:'Make my mark on a world that is mine, with rules simple enough that I can imagine anything.', verb:'mine and place blocks', first30:'You are dropped into an unfamiliar landscape with empty hands and only a corner toast telling you to punch a tree. Punching a tree for a few seconds knocks loose a block of wood, which the crafting grid turns into planks, then a workbench, then a wooden pickaxe. The light starts to fail, a groan sounds somewhere nearby, and the obvious next question, dig in or keep exploring, arrives from the world itself rather than from a menu.', minute:'Chase the vein of ore you can see, or turn back before the tunnel gets too dark to light? Chop one more tree for planks, or start building the shelter now while there is still light to see the walls go up? Fight the creature at the cave mouth, or wall it out and route around? Every choice spends the same two things: daylight, and whatever you are carrying that a fall or a fight could cost you.', engines:['creativity','discovery','expression','social'], why:'One primitive, the block, is both the terrain and the material, so anything the player can see they can eventually hold and place somewhere else; a tree, a mountain and a house are made of the same stuff. A short list of interacting systems, crafting, light level, hostile mobs, farming and later redstone, keeps generating new goals out of that one primitive rather than needing scripted content to hand them out. The day and night cycle supplies a beginner’s whole first goal, build something that keeps the dark out, before the player has read a single instruction, and once shelter is solved the sandbox has no further script, so the next goal is whatever the player decides to build. Notch released the first public build in May 2009 and began charging for it that June, adding features in response to a community that was already trading tips and building things he had not anticipated, and the game has stayed a canvas players fill with structures, mods and, through redstone, working calculators and computers nobody at Mojang designed.', complaints:'A player who wants a stated objective can bounce off the game in the first hour, since nothing on screen tells them shelter, then what; direction essentially runs out once the starter tools and a bed exist, and everything after is self-set. On PC, recipes were never explained in the game itself until a recipe book arrived in Java Edition 1.12 in 2017, five and a half years after the full release, so a generation of PC players learned crafting from wikis and friends, though the 2012 Xbox 360 Edition already listed every recipe. The block-by-block interface makes fine architectural detail slow, and survival’s hunger and mob pressure can feel like friction to a player who only wants to build.', lesson:'A single expressive primitive, one that is simultaneously the world’s scenery and its raw material, beats a long list of narrow, single-purpose features, because a handful of rules that interact keep producing situations nobody scripted, from a farm to a redstone calculator. Give a beginner one concrete, world-supplied goal, survive the first night, so the game teaches its basic verbs before it hands the player a blank page, then get out of the way and let the sandbox be a sandbox for the players who no longer need that goal.', misses:'Copies add many block types and crafting recipes but keep each one narrowly useful for a single recipe or structure, so nothing unexpected emerges from combining them the way ore, water, lava and redstone combine into Minecraft’s working machines. Others try to fix the lack of direction by bolting on a quest log or a fixed story, which answers the new player’s complaint but quietly removes the open canvas that keeps the sandbox’s own players around for years after the tutorial goals are gone.' },
  { id:'among-us', img:'assets/games/among-us.jpg', t:'Among Us', year:2018, genre:'social deduction', want:'Lie to my friends and catch them lying, with almost nothing to learn.', verb:'do tasks, accuse, vote (or sabotage and kill)', first30:'You spawn on the map with a personal task list and a colour, alongside as many as fourteen other crewmates. One or more of them, chosen at random and unknown to everyone else, is an Impostor who cannot do tasks and wants everyone else dead. The first minutes are quiet: crewmates scatter to their tasks, an Impostor pretends to do the same, and the game tells no crewmate who anyone else is.', minute:'Report this body now, or slip away before anyone sees who found it? Vouch for the crewmate you were just standing beside, or stay quiet and let someone else take the risk? As an Impostor: kill here, where a vent lets you disappear, or wait for a safer moment and a better alibi? Every minute is spent building or spending trust that nothing on screen can verify for you.', engines:['social','tension','surprise','expression'], why:'The whole rule set fits in a sentence, and the interesting content is always the other players: what they claim, who backs them up, and whether their story survives being checked. When the host leaves Visual Tasks on, a minority of tasks animate for any nearby witness, so completing one becomes real, checkable evidence rather than busywork, while an Impostor can only fake the motion. Reporting a body or calling an emergency meeting halts every other player’s screen at once and forces the same argument everyone else is having. The game’s success arrived two years after its 2018 launch, once streamers in South Korea and Brazil, then Sodapoppin on Twitch, made it visible during 2020 lockdowns, which shows the design was ready long before the audience found it.', complaints:'The game depends entirely on who else is in the lobby: a group of friends on a voice call produces a completely different experience from a room of silent strangers relying only on text chat, since Among Us ships with no built-in voice or proximity audio at all. Cheating, account bans evaded by rejoining, and players who leave mid-round rather than face a vote all disrupt matches strangers cannot easily prevent. New players can also find the growing list of roles added since 2021 confusing before they have learned the base game’s original two-role structure.', lesson:'When the players are the content, keep the rules small enough to learn in one match and spend the design budget on moments that generate a story worth retelling afterwards. Give at least some of the game’s busywork an objective witness, so an accusation can occasionally be checked rather than only argued, and design for spectatorship: a game clips and streams well when a stranger watching can follow what just happened without having played a single round themselves.', misses:'Copies add extra roles, abilities and permanent progression that make the rule set heavy before a new player has learned the base accusation-and-vote loop, diluting the one moment, a body, a meeting, a vote, that carries the whole game. Others copy the hidden-role structure but give every player identical, unwitnessable actions, so no accusation can ever be checked against anything, and the whole match collapses into unverifiable noise rather than an argument with real evidence on either side.' },
  { id:'wordle', img:'assets/games/wordle.svg', t:'Wordle', year:2021, genre:'daily word puzzle', want:'One small solvable thing a day, shared with everyone else doing the same one.', verb:'guess a five-letter word. Read the coloured feedback', first30:'A short How to play card opens on a first visit, with three example words showing what green, yellow and grey mean. Close it and an empty grid of six rows of five boxes waits above an on-screen keyboard. You type a five-letter guess and press enter, and the boxes flip one by one, left to right, confirming the card’s rule on your own letters.', minute:'Which letter do I test next: one already ruled out, or a fresh one that splits the remaining words in half? Do I play it safe and repeat a known green letter, or spend a guess purely to gather information about letters I have not tried yet? With only six rows and one puzzle a day, every guess is also a small bet against running out of attempts before running out of curiosity.', engines:['mastery','social','anticipation','flow'], why:'Josh Wardle revived a 2013 prototype during the pandemic as a private game for his partner, Palak Shah, who filtered his original word list of roughly thirteen thousand five-letter words down to about two thousand familiar ones so a shared puzzle would never feel unfair. Limiting everyone to the same one word a day, with no way to play again until tomorrow, turned a private habit into a synchronised one; when players began sharing hand-made grids of coloured squares to compare results without spoiling the answer, Wardle built that behaviour into a single share button, and daily players went from ninety on 1 November 2021 to more than two million by 9 January 2022 with no advertising, accounts or app store listing at all.', complaints:'Colour is the only feedback channel by default, so players with red-green colour blindness can struggle to tell a correct letter from a misplaced one until they dig into settings for the high-contrast mode. Words with a repeated letter can also mislead a quick glance, since a single colour per tile cannot show that a doubled letter is only partly confirmed. After the New York Times took over, a caching mismatch briefly let some players see a different word of the day than everyone else, and its editor’s later habit of choosing calendar-themed words for holidays drew complaints that curation was replacing the original list’s neutral randomness.', lesson:'A shared daily puzzle with no replay turns a private habit into a synchronised one, and a spoiler-free way to boast about the result, discovered by players rather than designed in from day one, can spread a game faster than any advertising. Watch what players invent to work around a missing feature, since building their own workaround into the product officially is often better design than guessing at it in advance.', misses:'Copies remove the daily limit and let players replay endlessly, which keeps the puzzle but loses the scarcity that made a single, shared result worth sharing at all. Others copy the name and grid to sell ads around them: a wave of App Store clones reused the Wordle name in January 2022 with minimal changes to play, and nearly all of them had been removed by the end of that month.' },
  { id:'hollow-knight', img:'assets/games/hollow-knight.jpg', t:'Hollow Knight', year:2017, genre:'metroidvania action', want:'Be lost in a vast, beautiful, hostile place and slowly make it mine through skill and attention.', verb:'strike, jump, dash. Explore and map', first30:'You drop onto a quiet cliffside path with a nail and nothing else: no map, no quest log, no marker. The first enemies are slow crawlers that teach the strike and the knockback of a hit. Past a near-empty town, a well leads down into the dark, and the only instruction is the space itself.', minute:'Push deeper with the Geo you are carrying, or go back to the last bench and risk nothing? Which charms suit this area’s enemies? Explore the unknown branch, or follow the corridor you half remember? Spend soul on a heal now, or save it for a spell?', engines:['discovery','mastery','tension','expression'], why:'Withholding the map until you find and buy it, and marking little on it, turns exploration into a skill the player visibly gets better at. Combat is simple to read (few moves, clear telegraphs) and hard to master, so every region can raise the stakes without new rules. The world tells its story through spaces and characters rather than cutscenes. The scope is large, but each area asks a distinct question, so the size reads as variety rather than padding.', complaints:'Getting lost frustrates players who want a clear next step, especially early, before the habit of finding Cornifer sets in. Some bosses are sharp difficulty spikes, and the walk back from a bench to a hard boss makes every retry slow. Objectives are unmarked, so a player who misses one clue can stall for a long time.', lesson:'Make exploration a skill by withholding the map, not by hiding the exits. Keep the cost of being lost real but bounded: frequent benches, a cheap first region, a way to recover what you dropped. Give each area a distinct movement or combat question, so memory has something to hold on to.', misses:'Copies ship a big map with markers everywhere, so exploration becomes checklist travel between icons. Or they copy the difficulty and the melancholy mood without the readable, short-telegraph combat and carefully placed benches, so the game is hard in ways the player cannot learn from.' },
  { id:'animal-crossing-nh', img:'assets/games/animal-crossing-nh.jpg', t:'Animal Crossing: New Horizons', year:2020, genre:'life sim', want:'A gentle place that changes every real day and is entirely mine to arrange.', verb:'gather, craft, decorate, visit', first30:'You land on a deserted island with a tent and two neighbours who already know your name, and the first evening’s only jobs are gathering branches and fruit for a welcome campfire. Next morning Tom Nook hands over a phone and a bill payable in Nook Miles, then leaves you alone: there is no quest marker and no timer. Picking up a stick or shaking a tree already earns something, so the first minutes teach that touching anything on the island pays back in a small, immediate way.', minute:'Fish this shadow in the river, or dig up the clam spurting water on the beach instead? Craft the recipe you just learned, or save the material for something rarer later? Spend today’s Nook Miles on a pocket upgrade, or let them sit until the total funds something bigger? Say yes to a villager’s small errand now, or come back once the shop closes and the island is quiet? None of these choices are timed, but the island’s clock keeps moving whether or not you act.', engines:['expression','collection','social','relief'], why:'Real-world time runs the whole island: the game reads the Switch’s own clock, so a shop opens and closes on a real schedule, a season turns on the real calendar date, and a fish or bug that only appears in one season vanishes for months if the player misses its window, which turns ordinary daily play into something that keeps producing new content for a whole year rather than being solved once. Tom Nook’s loans for the tent-to-house upgrades charge no interest and never carry a due date, so a player who ignores the debt for weeks loses nothing beyond the upgrade itself, which keeps the appearance of a mortgage without its real financial pressure. Because almost nothing else is required of the player, decorating, fishing, chatting or simply lying on the beach are all equally valid ways to spend a session, and beyond Isabelle’s star rating the island is judged by the person arranging it and whoever they invite to see it. The game’s March 2020 launch landed at the start of global pandemic lockdowns, and reviewers and players alike credited its calm, structureless pace with giving people a reason to keep a Switch on every day when very little else about that year had a fixed routine.', complaints:'A player who wants a fixed goal, a win state, a credits sequence, will not find one, and someone used to being told what to do next can spend their first sessions unsure whether they are making progress at all. Tool durability breaks a favourite axe or net at an inconvenient moment and sends the player back to a workbench, and early NookPhone menus stack several taps between wanting an item and using it. Because so much content is seasonal or calendar-gated, a slow starting island can feel thin for weeks at a time between the arrival of new villagers, fish or furniture, and reviewers returning a year on found island progression stopping after a few months, with Nook Miles reduced to an afterthought.', lesson:'Tie a game’s pace to the real calendar, not an internal counter, and scarcity and return both arrive for free: a fish that is only ever in season for eight real weeks gives a player an actual reason to open the game on a specific day, in a way no arbitrary daily login timer manufactures on its own. Strip away every optional goal and the game still holds a player’s attention, provided the small, unscheduled actions (fishing, digging, decorating) each pay back immediately and an audience of visiting friends exists to make personal expression matter to someone other than the player.', misses:'Copies borrow the daily-rhythm calendar and the decorating tools but skip the audience: a canvas nobody but its owner ever sees loses most of the reason New Horizons’ daily visits and shared Dream Addresses gave expression any weight. Others add a calendar without truly tying it to the real clock and season, so nothing is ever missed, and the scarcity that made a limited-season fish or a Saturday-only concert worth planning around quietly disappears; a rhythm with no real cost to skipping it stops pulling players back on any particular day.' },
  { id:'factorio', img:'assets/games/factorio.jpg', t:'Factorio', year:2020, genre:'automation', want:'Turn chaos into a machine that runs without me, then find the next bottleneck.', verb:'place, connect, automate', first30:'You wake beside the wreckage of your crashed ship with almost nothing. The first minutes are handwork: chop trees for wood, mine iron ore, copper ore, coal and stone by hand, then place and fuel a stone furnace to smelt your first plates. A burner mining drill dropping ore straight into a stone furnace turns that handwork into the game’s first automatic step, well within reach of the four ore patches the map generator guarantees in the starting area.', minute:'Which shortage is worse right now: not enough iron plates, not enough power, or a belt backed up because one inserter cannot keep pace? Build the next assembler, or rebuild this row to clear a jam? Push the perimeter out for more ore and risk the biters whose nest your pollution has now reached, or wall up and defend what is already built? Every minute trades one visible shortage against the next one it is about to expose.', engines:['optimization','mastery','creativity','flow'], why:'Solving one bottleneck, not enough iron plates, say, always exposes the next one, not enough power, or a belt jammed by a slow inserter, so the goal regenerates from the player’s own progress rather than from a level designer. Every system talks to every other: belts, power and pollution, which draws the biters, hostile creatures that stay passive until a factory’s own pollution cloud reaches their nest. Wube Software, founded in Prague by Michal Kovařík and Tomáš Kozelek, ran Early Access from February 2016 to a full release in August 2020, publishing a weekly Friday Facts devlog from 2013 to 1.0, and again from 2023 to 2024 and folding player feedback straight into the design. The Steam store page states plainly that the developers have no plans to discount the game for the foreseeable future, keeping the price itself part of the pitch. Wube kept building afterwards: the paid Space Age expansion, released alongside a free 2.0 update in October 2024, adds four more planets to automate.', complaints:'Reaching a factory that runs itself can take a new player many hours, and in-game help thins out as systems deepen: the tutorial covers the basics and the tips window demonstrates trains and circuits, but advanced logistics is still mostly learned from the wiki or from watching someone else’s factory. Biters divide opinion: some players want the pressure of defending a factory the pollution cloud has provoked, while others turn evolution off entirely and treat the whole game as a peaceful puzzle instead. A factory that grows across many hours of play can also slow a computer’s simulation speed long before the player has run out of things worth automating.', lesson:'Design a goal that regenerates from the player’s own success: solving one shortage should always create the next one rather than closing the game out. Let separate systems, production, power, pollution, defence, talk to each other, so a problem is never confined to one screen or one fix. And talk about the design in public while its direction is settled, as Wube’s weekly Friday Facts did for years, but go quiet when showing unfinished ideas would read as a promise.', misses:'Copies add more machine types without making them interact, so the bottleneck a player fixes today looks exactly like the one from an hour ago and stops surprising anyone. Others copy escalating danger on a fixed timer instead of tying it to the player’s own pollution, so aggression feels scripted rather than earned by how much the player has built. Few keep a weekly public devlog running for seven years before release, and fewer still refuse every discount once the game has become a hit.' },
  { id:'portal', img:'assets/games/portal.jpg', t:'Portal', year:2007, genre:'first-person puzzle', want:'Feel clever, constantly, with one tool, and be told a story while doing it.', verb:'place two portals. Walk through', first30:'You wake in a glass-walled Relaxation Vault while an announcer’s voice, GLaDOS, reads a string of clipped welcome and safety notices and counts down. A portal opens in the wall, you step through it, and the room you just left is now beside you. Nothing else is explained; the rule of the whole game is understood from that one step, with no menu, no text box and no tutorial prompt.', minute:'Where do you need to end up, and which wall can take a portal? Does momentum from a fall carry you far enough if you aim the exit portal sideways instead of down? Is the cube on the button, or still on the ledge where you left it at the other end of the chamber?', engines:['discovery','mastery','storytelling','surprise'], why:'A single mechanic, place an entry and an exit portal, was taught entirely by level design: each of the game’s twenty test chambers, numbered 00 to 19, tends to add one new idea, a wall that will not take a portal, a cube that must sit on a button, momentum carried through a fall, before the next chamber quietly combines it with what came before. The mechanic itself came from Narbacular Drop, a 2005 student project at DigiPen; Valve hired its whole team, including designer Kim Swift, after seeing it at the school’s career fair, and rebuilt the idea inside Aperture Science with GLaDOS as narrator. Valve’s own culture of weekly playtesting shaped the result directly: when testers ignored the Weighted Companion Cube meant to teach them to plan around a carried object, the team added dialogue and a heart icon rather than a tutorial prompt, and kept escalating from there.', complaints:'The whole campaign runs to only two or three hours, and once a player has learned the full vocabulary, entry and exit placement, momentum, cubes on buttons, turrets, later chambers mostly recombine the same ideas rather than introducing new ones, so the second half can feel like an extension of the first rather than fresh material. There is no difficulty setting, and the replay offered after the credits, six advanced chambers and challenge modes counting portals, footsteps and time, reworks the same rooms rather than adding new ideas, so many players never return after one pass.', lesson:'Teach a single mechanic entirely through the space the player stands in, one new idea per room, and let the fiction, a testing facility with an AI already running it, justify why every lesson arrives without a word of tutorial text. Keep the ruleset small enough that a two- or three-hour game can still end with the vocabulary feeling fully explored rather than only partly introduced, and treat a short, complete game as a legitimate goal in itself, not a sign of missing content.', misses:'Copies add a second and third mechanic before the first one has been fully explored across a room’s worth of chambers, so no single idea gets the isolated teaching treatment that made Portal’s early chambers legible on sight. Others explain a new rule with an on-screen prompt or a paragraph of text instead of a room built specifically to demonstrate it, which breaks the promise that the space itself is always the tutorial and pushes the player back into reading rather than testing.' },
  { id:'tetris', img:'assets/games/tetris.jpg', t:'Tetris', year:1984, genre:'falling-block puzzle', want:'Impose order on a stream of chaos, faster and faster, until I fail.', verb:'rotate and place a falling piece', first30:'A single tetromino appears at the top of an empty ten-wide well and starts to fall. You rotate and slide it before it lands, then a new one falls immediately after. Within half a minute a full row locks into place, vanishes, and every block above drops one line lower, so the whole rule is demonstrated without a word of instruction.', minute:'Where does this exact shape fit against the uneven skyline you have already built? Rotate now or slide first, since the two can put the same piece in different final columns? Clear this single line immediately for safety, or leave a gap open and risk the board to bank four lines at once for a tetris? Every answer only matters for the few seconds before the next piece already demands the same decision again.', engines:['flow','mastery','tension','optimization'], why:'Tetris teaches its whole rule set in the time it takes one piece to fall: rotate and place seven shapes so they lock into complete rows, which then clear. The only escalation is speed, so every session eventually produces the same verdict, a board the player can no longer read fast enough, which makes failure feel earned rather than imposed by the game. Because the rules need no language, no tutorial and no invented world, Tetris travelled from a Soviet research computer to arcades, consoles and phones on every continent with its rules intact, whatever dressing each publisher added, and by December 2024 had sold 520 million copies across versions, by most counts more than any other video game.', complaints:'Before the 2001 Guideline, Tetris used a purely random generator that could starve a player of the one piece they needed for many pieces in a row, so a loss could come from bad luck rather than a bad decision. Hypertapping, the technique that first pushed the NES version past level 29, a speed The New Yorker said seems intentionally impossible, means tensing the arm until the thumb trembles on the pad; rolling, the grip developed in 2020 that followed it, is both faster and less straining, but both ask far more of a hand than the game’s designers ever expected.', lesson:'A single escalating dial, speed alone, can carry a whole difficulty curve if the underlying decision, where does this shape go, stays readable at almost any pace. Keep the rule set small enough to learn from the first piece, and let the same seven shapes generate difficulty through pace rather than through added rules, so forty years and hundreds of licensed versions can all still be recognised as the same game.', misses:'Copies often change the shapes, add power-ups or attach a narrative layer on top of the falling blocks, which breaks the exact colour-to-shape recognition players have built and turns a reasoned placement back into a guess. Others keep the pieces but also keep a purely random generator, so a run can be lost to a drought of one shape rather than to the player’s own read of the board, the exact fairness problem the Guideline’s seven-bag system was written to remove.' }
];

